{"id":"35065931-5c3a-4838-a1c2-a2c2f218768a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785015664,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import json\\nfrom collections import deque\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    node_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    removed = set()\\n    all_ids = list(node_dict.keys())\\n    all_ids.sort(key=lambda x: (-node_dict[x]['depth'], x))\\n    q = deque(all_ids)\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while q:\\n        nid = q.popleft()\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_dict[nid]\\n        if len(children[nid]) == 0 and node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\\n            removed.add(nid)\\n            pid = node['parent']\\n            if pid is not None and pid not in removed:\\n                children[pid].remove(nid)\\n                q.append(pid)\\n    retained = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\\n    return {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that processes a tree of nodes (with parent references, removable flag, utility, etc.) and a utility_threshold. The task is to implement a process that simulates a queue of nodes to consider for removal (pruning). The description: \"Maintain and update the syntax-guided reduction work queue across accepted and rejected tree rewrites without revisiting invalidated nodes.\" But from the examples and the specific instructions, it seems more like: given a tree of nodes, we need to iteratively remove removable leaf nodes whose utility <= threshold, and after removal, enqueue its parent (if not already removed) for reconsideration. We must return the retained node IDs (i.e., those not removed) and the complete visit order (the order in which nodes were visited/processed, presumably the order we pop from queue). The queue should be initialized in descending depth order, and for nodes at same depth, stable node-ID order (string sort). We must skip nodes already removed (i.e., if a node is popped and it's not a leaf, or not removable, or utility > threshold, we just record visit order but not remove). After removal, enqueue its retained parent (if parent is not already removed) for reconsideration. We need to ensure we don't revisit invalidated nodes? Actually says \"without revisiting invalidated nodes\" – but the process seems to allow revisiting parent after child removal, but not revisiting nodes that have been removed. Also \"no_invalidated_revisit\" contract means we don't revisit nodes that have been removed. That's natural.\n\nWe need to produce two arrays: retained_node_ids (all node IDs that remain after removals) and visit_order (the order in which nodes were popped from the queue, i.e., processed). Note: The root (depth 0) has parent null and removable false. It should never be removed.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate that case contains \"nodes\" (list) and \"utility_threshold\" (float).\n2. Build a mapping from node id to node dict. Also build children list for each node to know if a node is a leaf (no children, or only children that are removed? Initially leaves are nodes with no children. But after removals, a node may become leaf if all its children have been removed). We need to track removal status.\n3. Initialize a queue list. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending. Use that order for initial queue. Note: The queue should contain node IDs (strings). We'll process in order.\n4. Maintain a set of removed nodes (initially empty). Maintain a set of retained nodes initially all nodes.\n5. Process queue: While queue not empty, pop first element (pop from front, using list as queue with pop(0) is O(n), better use collections.deque). For each popped node id:\n   - Append node id to visit_order.\n   - If node is already removed, skip (continue).\n   - Check if node is a leaf: a node is leaf if it has no children that are still retained (i.e., not removed). So we need children list that we update when a child is removed.\n   - Check if node is removable (removable==True) and utility <= threshold.\n   - If so, remove the node: add to removed set, remove from children list of its parent (if parent exists). Then enqueue the parent if parent is not null and parent is not removed (but parent might already be in queue? Need to add to queue for later processing). However, rule: \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" So we add parent id to the queue (end). But should we avoid duplicates? The queue may already have the parent. It's okay to have duplicates; we can process, but it's inefficient. Usually we can add only if not already queued? Not specified. Contract \"queue_order\" suggests deterministic order. Probably we should allow duplicates but that could lead to repeated processing. However, we skip if already removed, and if it's already processed, we might process again but it's not a leaf or not removable. But we must not cause infinite loop. Since we only add parent when a child is removed, and parent is not removed (since we only remove leaf nodes, parent is retained), and parent may become leaf later. Adding parent again is fine. But to avoid endless repetition, we need to ensure that after processing parent, if it still doesn't get removed, we don't add it again unless another child removal triggers. Since we only add on child removal, it's limited. However, if parent is already in queue (not yet popped), adding another copy might cause it to be processed multiple times. That could be okay but might cause extra entries in visit_order. The specification doesn't explicitly forbid duplicates in visit_order. But likely we want each distinct node to appear at most once in visit_order? Not necessarily, but typical BFS/DFS like processes have each node visited once. The description: \"complete visit order\" could mean the order in which we pop nodes from queue, which could include duplicates if re-enqueued. However, it might be simpler to only enqueue parent if it's not already in queue (or not already scheduled). But we don't have a set of queued items. I think it's safer to allow duplicates and rely on the \"skip nodes already removed\" to handle. But if parent is not removed and we process it again, we will again check if leaf, etc. This could lead to multiple visits if parent remains not removable. For example, root might be enqueued multiple times, but root is not removable, so every time we pop root we just record visit_order and skip. That would bloat visit_order. That seems not intended. Let's examine the process description: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order. Skip nodes already removed. Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold. After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration. Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" This suggests that each node should be visited (popped) exactly once initially, and then only when a parent is re-enqueued after child removal. That parent may be re-enqueued only once per removal event. But if parent later becomes leaf and still not removable, it could be revisited again after another child removal? Actually, if parent has multiple children, each removal could enqueue the parent again. That would cause multiple visits. However, after the first removal, the parent is not a leaf because it still has other children. After subsequent removals, parent becomes leaf eventually. So it makes sense to revisit parent each time a child is removed, to check if it has become leaf and if it is removable. So duplicate visits are plausible. But we must avoid infinite loops: parent may never be removable, so we keep adding it each time we remove a child, but each removal reduces children count. Suppose parent has k children, we remove each child one by one, each time enqueue parent. That results in k enqueues of parent. Then parent becomes leaf after k-th removal, but we still have one more enqueue. So the last enqueue will process parent as leaf. That seems correct. However, if parent is not removable even as leaf, after processing we don't enqueue anything else. So visit_order will contain parent id multiple times (one for each child removal). That might be intended.\n\nBut we have to be careful: The queue is initialized with all nodes (in depth order). Then we pop and process. When we remove a node, we enqueue its parent (if retained). That parent might already have been popped earlier or still be in queue. Adding to the end is fine. So eventual order will be: initial order, then intermixed with re-enqueued parents as they come.\n\nWe need to implement children tracking. Initially, build adjacency: for each node, children list (ids). When we remove a node, we remove it from its parent's children list (if parent exists). Also we should not attempt to remove a node that is not in retained set (since we skip removed). Also note: The root (parent null) should not be removed.\n\nWe need to compute retained_node_ids: all node ids that are not in removed set (including root). Return sorted? The spec says \"retained_node_ids\" is an array of strings. It does not specify order. It likely should be in the same order as nodes appear in input? Or stable? Probably any order is fine, but to be safe we can return them in the order they appear in the original nodes list, excluding removed ones. Or preserve natural order. Since not specified, we can sort by id or keep original order. I'll keep order of input nodes list, skip removed.\n\nVisit order: list of node ids in order popped from queue (including the ones that are skipped because removed, and including duplicates). That matches \"complete visit order\". But does it include nodes that are visited multiple times? Yes.\n\nNow implement.\n\nEdge cases: Nodes with parent that is null (root). Also nodes may have missing parent in the list? Assume valid.\n\nWe need to parse case as a dict.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    # Build dict\n    node_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children lists\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n.get('parent')\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n        else:\n            root_id = n['id']\n    removed = set()\n    # Initialize queue: sort by depth descending, then id ascending.\n    # depth is in node_dict.\n    all_ids = list(node_dict.keys())\n    all_ids.sort(key=lambda x: (-node_dict[x]['depth'], x))\n    from collections import deque\n    q = deque(all_ids)\n    visit_order = []\n    while q:\n        nid = q.popleft()\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        node = node_dict[nid]\n        # Check if leaf: no children (that are not removed)\n        # We need to check children list (which only contains retained children because we remove from it)\n        # So leaf if len(children[nid]) == 0\n        if len(children[nid]) == 0 and node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\n            # Remove it\n            removed.add(nid)\n            pid = node['parent']\n            if pid is not None and pid not in removed:\n                # Remove nid from parent's children list\n                children[pid].remove(nid)\n                # Enqueue parent\n                q.append(pid)\n    # retained_node_ids: all ids not in removed, preserve original order\n    retained = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\n    return {\"retained_node_ids\": retained, \"visit_order\": visit_order}\n\nCheck with examples? We don't have expected outputs but we can reason.\n\nTest with first fixture: nodes utility threshold 0.55. Let's simulate quickly.\n\nBut we need to ensure queue order: initially descending depth then id. Depth 0: root (depth0). Depth1: n1, n2, n3 (depth1). Depth2: n4, n5, n6 (depth2). So depth2 first: id order: n4, n5, n6 (alphabetically n4<n5<n6). Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. Then root.\n\nProcess:\nPop n4: leaf? children of n4 = [] initially. Is n4 removable? Yes (removable true). utility=0.2087 <= 0.55 -> remove. Add to removed. Parent is n2. Remove n4 from n2's children. Enqueue n2 (if not removed). n2 not removed yet. Queue becomes: after n4 popped, queue left: initial order after n4: n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root. Then append n2 -> now queue: n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root, n2.\nPop n5: leaf? children [] -> depth2, removable false -> not removable (removable false) so skip removal but record visit. Continue.\nPop n6: leaf, removable true, utility=0.352 <=0.55 -> remove. Parent n1. Enqueue n1. Remove n6 from n1's children. Now queue: n1, n2, n3, root, n2 (from earlier), n1.\nPop n1: leaf? children of n1 after removal: was empty? originally had n6, after removal children[n1] becomes []. So leaf, removable false (since n1 removable false) -> not removed.\nPop n2: Let's check children of n2 originally had n4, after removal children[n2] = [] (only n4). So leaf, removable true, utility=0.742 > 0.55 -> not removed.\nPop n3: children originally n5, still present (n5 not removed because not removable). So not leaf. removable false. skip.\nPop root: not leaf (has children n1,n2,n3 retained). Not removable.\nPop n2 (second time): now after previous processing, n2 was not removed, still leaf. But we haven't removed any other children, so n2 still leaf, utility>threshold, skip.\nPop n1 (second time): leaf, removable false, skip.\nQueue empty.\n\nSo retained: all nodes except n4 and n6 (removed). Visit order: [n4,n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root,n2,n1]. That seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to output implementation as JSON string under \"implementation\". Must be a valid Python source.\n\nWe must escape backslashes, quotes, newlines in the JSON string. We'll write the Python code as a string, then JSON-encode it.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The function must not mutate case. We are reading from case, but not modifying it. We create local structures.\n\nAlso note: The problem mentions \"syntax-guided reduction work queue\" etc. but from the test cases it's exactly this process.\n\nNow compose the answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2012,"completion_tokens":3358,"total_tokens":5370,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1920},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3038},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1920,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":92},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}